// A donated process slot is a process the machine runs.
//
// The transcript:
//
//   SOS procdonate: floor=3 third child refused: out of kernel objects
//   SOS procdonate: donated a region of process slots
//   SOS procdonate: third child created past the floor
//   SOS procdonate: started three children
//   SOS echo0: alive       <- each child, scheduled, in its own address space
//   SOS echo1: alive
//   SOS echo2: alive       <- this one lives in a donated slot
//   SOS procdonate: exits a=65577 b=65578 c=65579
//   SOS procdonate: donated slot reused d=65579
//   SOS procdonate: done
//
// A donated process slot must carry everything a process needs: its handle
// table, boot-handle set (with count and cursor) and quota rows are fields of
// `ProcessSlot`, not tables sized for the compiled floor. So a deployment that
// donates runs more concurrent processes than the image was built for.
//
// Five claims, in order.
//
//  1. The floor is real. `FLOOR` is `MAX_PROCESSES` and root is one of them,
//     so root plus `FLOOR - 1` children is the whole table. The next
//     `process_create` meets the slab and answers `NoResource`, a status,
//     because the caller cannot know how full the machine's table is.
//
//  2. The donation is ordinary memory, cut out of a region root was given.
//
//     The piece donated is a fresh cut, not a split's remainder. A region that
//     has lent bytes out is a pool root with `out_bytes != 0`, and donating one
//     faults (retiring a root under its own pieces would leave their
//     back-references naming a stranger). A freshly cut piece has lent nothing,
//     is named by exactly one handle and is mapped nowhere, so it satisfies all
//     three counts by construction.
//
//  3. The ceiling moved: the create that was refused now succeeds, and the
//     process it makes is the fourth live process on a machine compiled for
//     three.
//
//  4. A donated slot is a whole process. All three children are started and
//     each says its own line before exiting with its own code. That line is the
//     donated slot's handle table, quota ledger and boot set all working at
//     once: the child derives a Process object out of the System handle root
//     gave it (a boot-set record) and makes a syscall (a handle-table lookup).
//     The exit code coming back through root's wait record is its teardown.
//
//     The three codes are distinct, so `a=`/`b=`/`c=` is a claim about which
//     process ended, not merely that three did. They are read in key order
//     rather than completion order, so the line is deterministic however the
//     scheduler interleaves them.
//
//  5. The donated slot comes back and is spent again. Root drops the third
//     child's Process handle, which lets `reclaim_process_slot` free a `Gone`
//     slot. That slot is above the compiled floor, so the reclaim walk and the
//     allocator scan both have to reach into the donated extent. The
//     replacement child runs and dies exactly as the first one did, at a new
//     generation of the same slot.
//
// No slot count is printed, as in `tests/slab-donate`, `thread-donate` and
// `pipe-donate`: a donated region buys `len / sizeof(ProcessSlot)` slots, that
// size differs per profile, and every claim above is independent of it.
//
// The arm64 domain wall is why this case stops at four processes. A
// translating tier keeps a persistent table set per process slot in a static
// HAL pool that donation cannot reach, so `hal.PROT_DOMAIN_SLOTS` bounds what a
// donated slot can be used for there. It covers the fourth process on arm64;
// the next one is deliberately left unspent so the gate does not sit exactly
// on the wall. The wall's own refusal is not witnessed here.

import sos.{System, Process, Memory, SlabKind, SystemRight, Waiter, WaitResult,
            SosStatus}

static EXIT_OK: UInt = 0
static EXIT_FAILED: UInt = 1

/// `MAX_PROCESSES`, written down so that raising the kernel's floor fails this
/// case loudly rather than quietly making its first claim vacuous.
static FLOOR: UInt = 3

/// What each child's address space gets: part of the destination region the
/// build hands root. It holds an echo child's image and the stack the kernel
/// puts at the top with room to spare, and it is a multiple of every profile's
/// protection grain, which `split` requires.
///
/// It is cut off the front: `split` serves from the front, so the piece keeps
/// the destination row's own base, and on riscv32 a child image runs at exactly
/// the address it was linked at, so a child's region base is not a free choice.
static CHILD_SPAN: UInt = 0x20000

/// The donation: the next cut after the third child's span. Many process slots
/// on either profile and far more than this case spends, so the machine's
/// remaining bound is the tier's domain pool rather than the slab.
static GIFT: UInt = 0x2000

/// Root's config: the tag each child drains its System handle under. The kernel
/// gives it no meaning.
static TAG_BOOT: UInt = 61

/// The keys the three children are attached under, so the exits can be read in
/// a fixed order whatever order they happen in.
static KEY_A: UInt = 71
static KEY_B: UInt = 72
static KEY_C: UInt = 73

/// What a child may do: say a line, and find itself. Nothing else; a launcher
/// hands down the smallest set that works, and neither `ProcessCreate` nor
/// `SlabDonate` is in it.
///
/// `Transfer` is in the set because the give itself needs it (the
/// transfer-funnel contract): a capability that may not be transferred cannot
/// be handed down, so a sibling minted without this bit is one root can only
/// keep. Root's own set carries it and §3 attenuation is monotonic, so the
/// child's copy is the smallest set that can arrive at all.
static CHILD_SYSTEM: UInt32 = (SystemRight.Debug | SystemRight.ProcessSelf
                               | SystemRight.Transfer)

/// A status no arm of this case can produce honestly.
static BUG: SosStatus = SosStatus.Revoked

@export("_start")
func _start(boot_handle: UInt) {
    let system = System(boot_handle: boot_handle)

    let proc = try system.process_self() catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS procdonate: no process handle\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    // The build's region table, in `_region_rows`' order: every child's image
    // blob first, then every child's destination, both in the order the case
    // lists the packages. Root's business, never the kernel's.
    var img0_rec = try proc.boot_handle_next() catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS procdonate: no image 0\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    var img1_rec = try proc.boot_handle_next() catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS procdonate: no image 1\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    var img2_rec = try proc.boot_handle_next() catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS procdonate: no image 2\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    guard let img0 = img0_rec.take_memory() else {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS procdonate: image 0 is not a region\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    guard let img1 = img1_rec.take_memory() else {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS procdonate: image 1 is not a region\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    guard let img2 = img2_rec.take_memory() else {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS procdonate: image 2 is not a region\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    // ---- Claim 1: the compiled floor is real -------------------------------
    //
    // Root is process 0, so two children fill the table. Each child's region is
    // the front of its own destination row, which keeps its base equal to the
    // address its image was linked at on the identity-placement profile.
    let c0 = try make_child(&system, &proc, &img0) catch {
        print("SOS procdonate: first child failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let c1 = try make_child(&system, &proc, &img1) catch {
        print("SOS procdonate: second child failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    // Both of those images are spent (the kernel read them with its own
    // privilege at create and a child's bytes are its region's now), so root
    // stops naming them. `MAX_HANDLES` is small and this launcher holds
    // several Process handles, regions and a Waiter at its peak, which is
    // close enough to the table that the two rows are worth giving back.
    let _ = move img0
    let _ = move img1

    var dst2_rec = try proc.boot_handle_next() catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS procdonate: no destination 2\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    guard let dst2 = dst2_rec.take_memory() else {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS procdonate: destination 2 is not a region\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let piece2 = try dst2.split(len: CHILD_SPAN) catch {
        print("SOS procdonate: no third child region: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    match system.process_create(image: &img2, memory: &piece2) {
        case Ok(_) -> {
            let _ = system.debug_print(
                "SOS procdonate: a fourth process existed BEFORE the donation\n")
            let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
            return
        },
        case Err(e) -> print("SOS procdonate: floor={} third child refused: {}\n",
                             FLOOR, e),
    }

    // ---- Claim 2: the donation ---------------------------------------------
    //
    // A fresh cut rather than what is left of `dst2`: see the header. `dst2`
    // has lent bytes to `piece2` and is therefore a pool root the op refuses.
    let gift = try dst2.split(len: GIFT) catch {
        print("SOS procdonate: no region to donate: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    try system.slab_donate(kind: SlabKind.Processes, memory: move gift) catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS procdonate: donation refused\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS procdonate: donated a region of process slots\n")

    // ---- Claim 3: the ceiling moved ----------------------------------------
    let c2 = try system.process_create(image: &img2, memory: &piece2) catch {
        print("SOS procdonate: the donated extent gave nothing: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS procdonate: third child created past the floor\n")

    // ---- Claim 4: and a donated slot is a whole process --------------------
    try furnish_and_start(&system, &c0) catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS procdonate: first child would not start\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    try furnish_and_start(&system, &c1) catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS procdonate: second child would not start\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    try furnish_and_start(&system, &c2) catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS procdonate: third child would not start\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS procdonate: started three children\n")

    let waiter = try proc.waiter_create() catch {
        print("SOS procdonate: no waiter: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    // A death is a terminal level, so attaching after the fact still wakes.
    // That lets these three waits run in key order and makes the line below
    // deterministic however the three children interleaved.
    let a = try reap(&waiter, &c0, KEY_A) catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS procdonate: first child never ended\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let b = try reap(&waiter, &c1, KEY_B) catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS procdonate: second child never ended\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let c = try reap(&waiter, &c2, KEY_C) catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS procdonate: third child never ended\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    print("SOS procdonate: exits a={} b={} c={}\n", a, b, c)

    // ---- Claim 5: the donated slot comes back, and is spent again ----------
    //
    // Dropping the handle is the release, and it lets the kernel reclaim a
    // `Gone` slot. This one lives above the compiled floor, so both the reclaim
    // and the next allocator scan have to reach into the donated extent.
    let _ = move c2

    let c2b = try system.process_create(image: &img2, memory: &piece2) catch {
        print("SOS procdonate: the donated slot did not come back: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    try furnish_and_start(&system, &c2b) catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS procdonate: replacement would not start\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let d = try reap(&waiter, &c2b, KEY_C) catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS procdonate: replacement never ended\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    print("SOS procdonate: donated slot reused d={}\n", d)

    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS procdonate: done\n")
    let _ = proc.exit(EXIT_OK)
    let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
}

/// Drain one destination row, carve the child's span off its front, and create.
///
/// The destination remainder is dropped on the way out: root has no further use
/// for it, and a region root stops naming is bytes the child's own grant rows
/// still hold, so nothing is lost and nothing is freed early.
func make_child(system: &System, proc: &Process,
                image: &Memory) -> Result<Process, SosStatus> {
    var dst_rec = try proc.boot_handle_next()
    guard let dst = dst_rec.take_memory() else { return BUG }
    let piece = try dst.split(len: CHILD_SPAN)
    return system.process_create(image: &image, memory: &piece)
}

/// Give a child the smallest System handle that lets it speak, and start it on
/// that record.
///
/// `start(boot_tag:)` puts the word in the child's first argument register,
/// which is the one handle §12 keeps at the entry; everything else a process is
/// furnished with is collected. One give and one start is the whole furnishing
/// an echo child needs.
func furnish_and_start(system: &System, child: &Process) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
    let sib = try system.mint(rights: CHILD_SYSTEM)
    try child.give(system: move sib, tag: TAG_BOOT)
    return child.start(boot_tag: TAG_BOOT)
}

/// Attach `child` under `key`, wait for its death, and answer the §8 status
/// word.
///
/// The attachment is removed afterwards so the next call can reuse the key,
/// which makes the three reads happen in a fixed order rather than in whichever
/// order the children finished.
func reap(waiter: &Waiter, child: &Process, key: UInt) -> Result<UInt, SosStatus> {
    try waiter.add(process: &child, key: key)
    let gone = try waiter.wait()
    if gone.key != key { return BUG }
    let status = status_of(&gone)
    let _ = move gone
    try waiter.remove(key)
    return status
}

/// The §8 status word out of a wait record.
func status_of(r: &WaitResult) -> UInt {
    match r.what {
        case Process(status) -> status,
        case _ -> panic("this launcher attached only Processes"),
    }
}
